{"type":"decision","classification":"tactical","recorded_at":"2026-05-31T23:38:20.148Z","evidence":{"commit":"14896b44b027a894f87fb39dd442e8823e402f16"},"relates_to":["mx-7130a1"],"title":"App trusts the gateway for JWT signature; verifySignature reserved, not built","rationale":"The app sits behind a validating gateway (APISIX openid-connect verifies the token against the realm JWKS before forwarding the Bearer), so the library's JwtClaimsSubjectExtractor does NOT re-verify the signature: it base64url-decodes the payload only and does structural + exp checks. This keeps opa-abac-core/-spring-security lean (no oauth2-resource-server, no nimbus/jjwt, no JWKS/key-rotation handling) and is the lean two-layer thesis (mx-7130a1). TRADEOFF, documented loudly: this is safe ONLY behind a validating gateway; deployed gateway-less / internet-exposed it is a vulnerability. A verifySignature=true property is RESERVED (a forward-looking config key) but deliberately UNIMPLEMENTED in this slice — the escape hatch for a gateway-less deployment. Verified end to end: a token with a garbage signature still extracts (the unit test asserts this), and the e2e matrix runs behind APISIX. Do not bolt signature verification on without also reconsidering whether the gateway should remain the trust anchor.","id":"mx-57c3fc","status":"archived","archived_at":"2026-07-16T13:49:12.618Z","archive_reason":"manual: opa-abac domain decomposition 2026-07-16 (see vault note); superseded/one-off, per prune assessment"}
{"type":"decision","classification":"tactical","recorded_at":"2026-06-04T20:49:39.943Z","evidence":{"commit":"2f7b675549a962a1085386b18adff8e16fcbb78d"},"relates_to":["mx-506f5c"],"title":"NEXT TOPIC: hierarchical resource authorization (ancestor-walk) — the deferred Phase-5-adjacent slice, not yet designed","rationale":"Phase 5 (DATA-FILTERING) is SHIPPED and merged to main (PR #11 squash 473fde8; docs moved to docs/to-do/implemented/DATA-FILTERING via PR #12 / 2f7b675). The maintainer's NEXT topic is HIERARCHY ('how do we handle hierarchy'), to be planned AFTER a context compaction. Starting context for that design session: (1) WHAT IT IS — a child resource inheriting authorization from an ancestor (Catalog -> Category -> Product tree): a grant on an ancestor satisfies a check on a descendant. The prior-platform pattern is mx-506f5c (resolve the parent chain; make 'hierarchical' an EXPLICIT opt-in mode on the authorization API, not implicit, so callers know when ancestor grants apply). (2) WHY IT'S SEPARATE FROM PHASE 5 — ADR 0005/0006 explicitly DEFERRED it: Phase 5 filters by the row's OWN tags; hierarchy is 'a different mechanism (load-then-walk-parents), larger'. (3) WHAT ALREADY STUBS IT — the example app's CategoryAuthorizer + CategoryListAuthorizer already do a ONE-STEP hierarchy: they resolve the role on the GOVERNING PARENT (a Category is governed by its Catalog's team -> resolve on (catalog, category.catalogId), not the category id). That hard-coded single step is what the general N-level ancestor-walk generalizes. The load-then-check seam is mx-76e16f. (4) OPEN DESIGN QUESTIONS for the session: where the walk lives (library vs example — the one-step is currently example-app code on public beans); how it composes with the Phase-5 list filter (does a list endpoint inherit ancestor grants?); explicit opt-in shape (an @OpaPreAuthorize flag? a HierarchicalAuthorizer bean? a rego ancestor-join?); and whether it overlaps Phase 8 ReBAC-in-Rego (the team/membership/grant graph in policy). (5) PROCESS — follow the established slice ritual: a full planning package under docs/to-do/planning/<SLICE>/ (index + 00-DESIGN + 01-DECOMPOSITION + AUTONOMOUS-IMPLEMENTATION-PROMPT + 10-QA + STATUS stubs), an ADR written UP FRONT, branch feature/void3110/<slice>, identity Void3110 <void31102025@gmail.com>, clean-room, Mulch sync .mulch-only. Repo state at handoff: on main, clean tree, all green (gradle build, opa test 60/60, CI).","id":"mx-5d2464","status":"archived","archived_at":"2026-07-16T13:49:12.618Z","archive_reason":"manual: opa-abac domain decomposition 2026-07-16 (see vault note); superseded/one-off, per prune assessment"}
{"type":"decision","classification":"tactical","recorded_at":"2026-06-07T22:07:44.956Z","evidence":{"commit":"0eca3244629aa3cb9b3a9f0d21bd397b9672853a"},"relates_to":["mx-6876cd"],"supersedes":["mx-5d2464"],"title":"NEXT TOPIC: Slice 5.5-B — hierarchy-aware LIST filter (subtreeSpec composed with the Phase-5 residual). 5.5-A is shipped+merged.","rationale":"Slice 5.5-A (single-resource N-level hierarchical authorization) is SHIPPED and merged to main (PR #14 squash 0eca324; package at docs/to-do/implemented/HIERARCHY-SINGLE-RESOURCE/; guide docs/guides/HIERARCHICAL-AUTHORIZATION.md). Repo at handoff: on main, clean tree, all green (./gradlew build, opa test 72/72, CI build(21), all 5 newman gateway matrices). The maintainer's NEXT topic is Slice 5.5-B: make the Phase-5 partial-eval LIST filter hierarchy-aware so a grant on an ancestor WIDENS which rows a list endpoint (e.g. GET .../products or .../categories) returns — not just single-resource checks. DESIGN IS ALREADY PINNED by ADR 0008 (docs/architecture/adr/0008-hierarchical-resource-authorization.md, 'How it composes with the Phase-5 list filter'): the OPA residual STAYS TAG-ONLY (CompileResponseParser / ResidualSpecificationFactory / the closed operator set are UNCHANGED — 'is this row in an allowed subtree' is a fact about LINEAGE, not tags), and hierarchy widening is a SEPARATE, APP-BUILT subtreeSpec OR-ed into the query: combined = scope.and( tagResidual.or(subtreeSpec) ).and( notDenied ). subtreeSpec = ltree 'path <@ <allowed-subtree-roots>' (the LtreeAncestorResolver/path already shipped in 5.5-A) OR 'id IN (<descendant-id-set>)' (CTE), where the allowed-subtree-roots are resolved ONCE per list call from the subject's inheritable ancestor grants. FAIL-CLOSED by construction: if hierarchy resolution fails, subtreeSpec is an empty (false) predicate and the result falls back to the NARROWER tag-only filter — never wider. WHAT 5.5-B BUILDS ON (all shipped in 5.5-A): the ltree path column + GiST index + the AncestorResolver SPI + AbstractHierarchicalEntity + the inheritable config + opt-in default-off. The single-resource HierarchicalAuthorizer stays as-is. WHERE THE SEAM LIVES: AbacQueryService is the Phase-5 list seam (findAuthorized: compile->residual->Specification->findAll, scope.and(authzSpec)); 5.5-B adds the subtreeSpec OR-branch there or in an app-level CategoryListAuthorizer-style composer (CategoryListAuthorizer currently does tag-only list filtering and was deliberately UNTOUCHED in 5.5-A). OPEN QUESTIONS for the design session: (a) does subtreeSpec live in the library (a HierarchicalSubtreeSpecFactory in spring-data) or app-side; (b) how allowed-subtree-roots are computed (which ancestor grants the subject holds that are inheritable to the listed type) without a per-row resolve; (c) deny-overrides on lists (a denied leaf must drop out even if a subtree grant would include it); (d) the e2e shape (two subjects, same list endpoint, different subtree row sets + a denied carve-out). Per-node independent grants remain Phase 8 (ReBAC). PROCESS: the established slice ritual — grill-me if useful, an ADR 0009 ONLY if a list-specific fork surfaces (else ADR 0008 governs), a full planning package under docs/to-do/planning/HIERARCHY-LIST-FILTER/ (the [[HIERARCHY-LIST-FILTER]] wikilink is already an intentional forward stub from the 5.5-A index), the slice-planner skill for decomposition, branch feature/void3110/hierarchy-list-filter, identity Void3110 <void31102025@gmail.com>, clean-room, Mulch sync .mulch-only.","id":"mx-69ba87","status":"archived","archived_at":"2026-07-16T13:49:12.618Z","archive_reason":"manual: opa-abac domain decomposition 2026-07-16 (see vault note); superseded/one-off, per prune assessment"}
{"type":"decision","classification":"tactical","recorded_at":"2026-06-09T18:59:29.585Z","evidence":{"commit":"897904c95ad1e4739b0973b5bcefdc66c6ccbdce"},"relates_to":["mx-3b45b7"],"title":"NEXT: Phase 5.9 REST API refinement (publication-readiness) BEFORE Phase 6 action enrichment","rationale":"Slice 5.5-B (hierarchy-aware list filter) is SHIPPED + MERGED to main (PR #21; Phase 5.5 complete: 5.5-A single-resource + 5.5-B list filter). The maintainer chose to insert a REST API refinement step BEFORE action enrichment, driven by docs/code-review/REST-API-DESIGN-REVIEW.md. That review found the API is sound and consistent with NO fail-open; the gaps are maturity/publication-readiness items. SCOPE (the review's priority-ordered follow-ups): (1) MEDIUM the publication item: RFC-7807 application/problem+json + a machine-stable errorCode enum so clients branch on a code not a localizable message (currently ApiError{status,message,timestamp} as application/json); (2) LOW Location header on every 201 (the id is already known); (3) LOW-MEDIUM one-line 'bootstrap: pre-membership, authenticated-only by design' intent comments at the deliberately-ungated user-service mutations (UserController, POST /teams) so an absent @OpaPreAuthorize reads as a decision; (4) DEFERRED a shared pagination envelope (a library-wide slice when volume justifies, NOT per-endpoint); (5) Phase-6 the actions/pageActions affordance metadata stays action-enrichment. Sequenced before Phase 6 so the _actions envelope lands on an already-RFC-7807-clean surface. Added to POC-ROADMAP as Phase 5.9. NEXT ACTION when work resumes: run the planning ritual — grill-me to settle the slice scope (how far to take problem+json; pagination in or deferred), then an ADR for the error-contract decision, then the slice-planner decomposition package, mirroring how 5.5-B was planned. The guide to refine against is docs/guides/REST-API-DESIGN.md (§3 status codes, §9 targets).","id":"mx-5bc284","status":"archived","archived_at":"2026-07-16T13:49:12.618Z","archive_reason":"manual: opa-abac domain decomposition 2026-07-16 (see vault note); superseded/one-off, per prune assessment"}
{"type":"decision","classification":"tactical","recorded_at":"2026-06-13T06:37:58.060Z","evidence":{"commit":"6c31fdec4a6535e9282483468287b0366e499349"},"title":"Route to publish (settled 2026-06-13): B2 fix-slice -> 6.7 (+control-plane B2 design folds in) -> Phase 6 enrichment -> Phase 7 publish","rationale":"After 6.5 merged (PR #34), the maintainer set the route to publication. SETTLED ORDER: (1) a STANDALONE B2 fix-slice FIRST — the supplier-outage error-distinct posture (resolve outage currently rides the realm fallback and, post-6.5, erases denied_actions/required_tags narrowing); fixed on its own small branch (distinguish outage->deny from no-role->fallback at the SPI contract), isolating the security fix with its own design+review rather than folding into 6.7. (2) Phase 6.7 control-plane vocabulary categorization — team:* verbs, TeamRoleCapabilities, the resolve-side team-verb injection ladder, and the define-tags ENFORCEMENT 6.5 deferred (Epic G/Story G4: ships in expansion math only today). Closes the one visible 'shipped-in-math-not-enforced' gap; the natural last correctness slice before publish. Inputs: ADR 0007 + the 6.5 addendum; PERMISSION-CATEGORIES 00-DESIGN decisions 1/5/8 (the deferred surfaces). Needs its own phase-① (escalation analysis for categorized control-plane power is a different problem from the catalog-domain taxonomy). (3) Phase 6 action enrichment — sequenced AFTER 6.7 per the maintainer (a more complete feature story before publish; affordance metadata is the portfolio/consulting differentiator); consumes 5.97 attributes + the 6.5 vocabulary, independent of 6.7. (4) Phase 7 publish prep. Small tracked items (OPA authn/trust docs, CI-runs-e2e, runner OPA-restart hygiene) fold into Phase 7 polish unless a slice touches them first. NOTE this REORDERS the roadmap's old 6->6.7 to 6.7->6.","id":"mx-9df34e","status":"archived","archived_at":"2026-07-16T13:49:12.618Z","archive_reason":"manual: opa-abac domain decomposition 2026-07-16 (see vault note); superseded/one-off, per prune assessment"}
{"type":"failure","classification":"tactical","recorded_at":"2026-06-17T15:46:18.043Z","evidence":{"commit":"f871b13c5f0b0a8c6b84fbb1993e890cc3f8b7dd"},"relates_to":["mx-fc941c"],"description":"In a Spring Boot starter, gating a nested auto-config on @ConditionalOnBean(SomeBean.class) where SomeBean is produced by ANOTHER nested auto-config bean (not user-supplied) is order-fragile and silently fails: the condition is evaluated before the producing bean is registered, so the dependent config registers nothing and no error is raised. Hit in Phase 6 T4: ActionEnrichmentAutoConfiguration gated on @ConditionalOnBean(AbacResourceCache.class), but the cache bean is produced by ResourceResolutionAutoConfiguration — the advice bean silently never registered (caught only because an ApplicationContextRunner test asserted bean presence).","resolution":"Gate the dependent config on the USER-SUPPLIED feature trigger, not the auto-config-produced bean. Here: @ConditionalOnBean(AbacResourceResolver.class) — the same condition that PRODUCES the cache, and the resolver is the app-supplied bean (reliably present when registered), exactly how the cache bean itself is gated. This both fixes the ordering hazard and is semantically correct (no resolver => no resolved attributes => nothing to enrich). GENERAL RULE: @ConditionalOnBean is reliable against user/withUserConfiguration beans and @ConditionalOnClass; it is NOT reliable against beans from sibling auto-config classes — gate on the shared upstream trigger instead. Always assert auto-config bean presence with an ApplicationContextRunner test, never assume registration.","id":"mx-e30125","status":"archived","archived_at":"2026-07-16T13:49:12.618Z","archive_reason":"manual: opa-abac domain decomposition 2026-07-16 (see vault note); superseded/one-off, per prune assessment"}
{"type":"failure","classification":"tactical","recorded_at":"2026-07-12T11:54:29.754Z","evidence":{"commit":"ee5bca37b930c572b0b115347a4d16df9417c338"},"relates_to":["mx-07ee10"],"description":"The load-testing rig (scripts/load, ADR-0021) needs a QUIESCENT macOS host, and 'quiet' is stricter than most assume — merely stopping the co-running podman IdP stack is NECESSARY BUT NOT SUFFICIENT. On 2026-07-12 two full REPS=3 runs both self-aborted RC=99 despite the app being provably healthy. The tell: gate-overhead guarded warmup+rep1+rep2 were pristine (p50 ~5.8ms, p90 ~7.3ms, p99 10-17ms, 0% fail, 0 dropped, exactly 50rps sustained) and then a LATER rep collapsed (p95 5.7s, p99 16.5s, 11% fail, 927 dropped iterations, throughput falling to 33/s). k6's open-model constant-arrival-rate executor caps at maxVUs=150; when host contention spikes tail latency, k6 can't init enough VUs to hold the target rate → it DROPS iterations, which run-load.sh scores as failures → the whole multi-rep run REDs on one bad rep. The app never degrades 6ms→16s between identical reps — the variance is 100% desktop background load: the podman applehv VM (8 vCPU/24GiB) while up, and AFTER stopping it, Spotify (~81% CPU), Spotlight/mds re-indexing (freed disk after the VM stop triggers a re-scan), and WindowServer, with free memory swinging 148MB–2.5GB. ADR-0021 'no invalid number is ever recorded' held both times: PERFORMANCE.md untouched, results/<ts>-full/ has only the gate-overhead summaries before the abort.","resolution":"To get a VALID full perf run on this laptop: (1) podman machine stop podman-machine-default (frees 8 vCPU/24GiB AND clears the :8181/:28181 OPA port collision); (2) QUIT Spotify and any other heavy renderer/media app; (3) let Spotlight settle (mdutil -s / wait for mds to drop off top) — stopping the podman VM frees disk which triggers a re-index; (4) ideally run when NOT actively using the laptop (WindowServer load matters). THEN from scripts/load: REPS=3 ./run-load.sh full && ./run-load.sh ceiling. Reading k6 v2.0.0 summary.json: the REAL failure rate is metrics.http_req_failed.value (NOT the confusing 'fails' count, which is the raw failing-check tally); percentiles are metrics.http_req_duration.{med,p(90),p(95),p(99)}; dropped_iterations.count>0 is the saturation signal. A single clean guarded gate-overhead baseline is already twice-established (~5.8ms p50 / ~15ms p99, guarded, 50rps) — trustworthy as the gate cost; what still needs a clean sweep is the two-pass ENABLE_OPA=0 unguarded DELTA and the ceiling ladder. Do NOT run heavy sub-agents / gradle concurrently with a perf run (that was the first run's extra contributor).","id":"mx-34177f","status":"archived","archived_at":"2026-07-16T13:49:12.618Z","archive_reason":"manual: opa-abac domain decomposition 2026-07-16 (see vault note); superseded/one-off, per prune assessment"}
{"type":"reference","classification":"observational","recorded_at":"2026-07-12T17:15:16.544Z","evidence":{"commit":"16bdc6b8763203e95e670109034b28afffd8cea5"},"name":"Pre-publish UI QA (demo SPA) results 2026-07-12","description":"Executed the A–I pre-publish UI QA against the live rig via the in-app browser (agent-driven; demo-fixture logins editor/demo/viewer/outsider/alice, password==username). ALL executed cases PASS. Persona-map reality (seed): editor=team OWNER, demo=demo-editor(L20 write+tag), viewer=demo-viewer(L10 read), outsider=no membership; alice provisioned live via directory. Proven live: B2 outsider empty list + B3 deep-link single-GET 403 (tenant isolation, membership sole access path); E1/E2/E3 amber(client-predicted)-vs-red(server)-vs-enforcement (forced create still 403); F2 dictionary tag editor on all 3 types + F3 illegal-value 422 + I2 client can't set operator-only abac_deny key (422 unknown key); G1 live Keycloak directory search + G2 self-service grant loop CLOSED (grant alice->reader, re-login, catalog now visible); D3/G3 custom non-owner role is management-incapable (role-change PUT->403); H1/H2 every denial RFC-7807 problem+json with typed errorCode. DIRECTORY GOTCHA: ENABLE_SPA=1 does NOT enable the directory (OPA_ABAC_DIRECTORY_KEYCLOAK_ENABLED stays false -> NoOpUserDirectory -> 'No directory accounts match'); G1/G2 need ENABLE_DIRECTORY=1 ENABLE_SPA=1 re-up (recreates pods, re-seed after). DEF-1 (cosmetic, non-security): SPA fires two concurrent POST /api/v1/users on first login (201 then 409) surfacing an alarming 'provisioning failed' amber banner though it SUCCEEDED; root cause App.tsx:139-143 provision useEffect has no in-flight guard (sibling PKCE bootstrap App.tsx:48-51 does), React19 StrictMode dev double-invoke -> largely a DEV artifact (prod build doesn't double-invoke). Fix: single-flight guard and/or treat 409-on-self-provision as success. Report: docs/code-review/PRE-PUBLISH-UI-QA-2026-07-12.md.","id":"mx-f49e25","status":"archived","archived_at":"2026-07-16T13:49:12.618Z","archive_reason":"manual: opa-abac domain decomposition 2026-07-16 (see vault note); superseded/one-off, per prune assessment"}
{"type":"failure","classification":"observational","recorded_at":"2026-07-12T20:55:09.141Z","evidence":{"commit":"3be58deb5cbe85b01ef35dea7fd00627d3ba632a"},"description":"SYMPTOM: new user's first SPA login flashed 'Profile provisioning failed (409)' amber banner though provisioning SUCCEEDED. CAUSE: example-demo-ui/src/App.tsx provisioning useEffect had no in-flight guard; React 19 StrictMode dev double-invokes effects -> ensureUser ran twice; ensureUser's resolve-then-create is check-then-act so both missed the ?subject lookup and both POSTed /api/v1/users -> 201 + 409. The sibling PKCE bootstrap effect (App.tsx module-scope 'bootstrap' promise) already had the guard; provisioning didn't. LESSON: any provisioning/one-shot effect in a StrictMode app needs a single-flight guard OR an idempotent-under-race backend call — do both. Largely a DEV artifact (prod build doesn't double-invoke StrictMode). Found by pre-publish UI QA, not by ITs (client-only race).","resolution":"Two-layer fix (PR #79): (1) App.tsx module-scope 'provisioning' Map<subject,Promise> single-flights the provisioning useEffect per subject — same once-only idiom as the PKCE 'bootstrap' guard; (2) api.ts ensureUser catches ApiError 409 on create and re-resolves the row (findUserBySubject) as success. Verified live: fresh user carol -> clean login, one GET ?subject 200 + single POST 201, no 409, console clean. tsc -b && vite build green.","id":"mx-d874c1","status":"archived","archived_at":"2026-07-16T13:49:12.618Z","archive_reason":"manual: opa-abac domain decomposition 2026-07-16 (see vault note); superseded/one-off, per prune assessment"}
